pages.content:
In **private inheritance**, a derived class inherits the public and protected members of the base class as **private**. This means those members are accessible **within the derived class**, but **not outside it**—not even through an object of the derived class.

Private inheritance is used when you want to **reuse** the functionality of a base class **without exposing** the inherited members as part of the derived class's interface.


**Syntax**

```cpp
class DerivedClass : private BaseClass {
    // Members of DerivedClass
};
```

---

**Example**

```cpp
#include 
using namespace std;

// Base class
class Machine {
public:
    void start() {
        cout << "Machine starting..." << endl;
    }

protected:
    void service() {
        cout << "Machine servicing..." << endl;
    }
};

// Derived class using private inheritance
class Printer : private Machine {
public:
    void printDocument() {
        start();       // Allowed: inherited as private
        service();     // Allowed: inherited as private
        cout << "Printing document..." << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Printer p;
    p.printDocument();     // Valid

    // p.start();           Error: start() is private in Printer due to private inheritance
    // p.service();         Error: service() is private in Printer

    return 0;
}
```


**Output**

```default
Machine starting...
Machine servicing...
Printing document...
```

